WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.400
Coat Divoir becomes the 75th nation to sign the Artemis Accords, expanding international commitments for lunar operations. This is Fascinating Frontiers, Episode 204. Coat Divoir sign the Artemis Accords through an official NASA process. The country is now the 75th to join the agreement. Officials there are committed to principles for safe, peaceful, and prosperous space activities. NASA released the formal announcement confirming the step.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:34.300
Signatories agree to common standards for exploration on the moon and beyond.

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:38.320
The addition brings another partner into coordinated lunar planning.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:43.080
Next up, a Dragon spacecraft reached pad 40 at Cape Canaveral.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.560
The arrival readies the vehicle for the crew 13 mission.

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:51.740
Four astronauts will head to the International Space Station for a long duration expedition.

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:55.520
Space X released imagery of the spacecraft at the pad.

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:59.120
The company confirmed the launch target remains on schedule.

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:04.980
On a different note, SpaceX completed a full launch rehearsal for Starship Flight 14

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:05.760
at Starbase.

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:10.060
The test sequence verified vehicle systems and grounded operations.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:14.320
Engineers checked every step needed for the next integrated flight.

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:17.560
The company shared video of the rehearsal activities.

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:21.840
This milestone moves the vehicle closer to its planned launch window.

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:28.980
Shifting focus, NASA astronaut Victor Glover signed with the creative artist's agency. The deal

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:34.940
covers representation in television, books, speaking, and endorsements. Glover retired from

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:40.960
active flight status after finishing his NASA assignments. He piloted the first operational crew

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:47.120
dragon mission to the space station. Glover also served as pilot on the Artemis II flight around the moon.

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:52.020
He joined California Polytechnic State University as an executive advisor.

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:57.920
Now turning to developments in China, engineers there are testing reusable rocket stages.

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:02.180
The stages return to landing sites after completing orbital missions.

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:05.840
Recent flights demonstrated controlled descent sequences.

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:10.320
The vehicles also performed engine-relight sequences after separation.

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:15.060
These steps narrow the gap with established reusable systems from other nations.

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:20.420
The Artemis Accords established shared guidelines for resource use on the lunar surface.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:24.300
The rules also cover safety zones and debris mitigation.

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:28.820
Multiple nations have adopted the framework to coordinate future landings.

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:33.500
The agreement adds practical operational rules to the outer space treaty.

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:37.520
Infrastructure planning now follows these coordinated standards.

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:42.760
SpaceX will launch a prototype satellite carrying Google Tensor Processing units.

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:48.200
The mission evaluates how the chip's handled machine learning tasks in the space environment.

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:52.440
Engineers will test performance under radiation and thermal stress.

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:55.880
Launch is scheduled aboard a Falcon 9 from Florida.

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:00.500
Data will inform future on-orbit artificial intelligence applications.

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:05.620
Israel Chairman S. Somanath presented a timeline running to 2040.

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:10.120
The plan includes reusable launch vehicles and a crude lunar landing.

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:13.620
A space station is also targeted within that period.

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:17.640
The roadmap emphasizes incremental technology demonstrations.

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:21.540
The Gaganyan program serves as the first major step.

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:26.920
Students at the Indian Institute of Technology Delhi heard the details during a public lecture.

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:32.340
The European Space Agency partnered with the European Commission and Museo Galileo.

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:38.880
The exhibition links Galileo Galilei's observations to the modern Galileo satellite navigation system.

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:44.320
Artifacts from the 17th century sit alongside models of current European satellites.

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:46.560
The display runs in Florence.

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:50.740
It highlights continuity in European space efforts across centuries.

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:55.280
Google will fly tensor processing units aboard a prototype satellite.

00:03:55.780 --> 00:03:59.300
The test measures radiation effects and thermal performance of the chips.

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:04.680
Engineers aim to prove the chips can support onboard artificial intelligence applications.

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:08.560
data from the flight will guide future constellation designs.

00:04:09.020 --> 00:04:12.300
The mission validates hardware for extended orbital operations.

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:17.040
I ride satellites captured an infrared image of the Venetian lagoon.

00:04:17.740 --> 00:04:20.760
The data were collected on May 17th, 2026.

00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:26.060
The images reveal temperature variations across channels and islands in northern Italy.

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:30.840
ESA released the image as part of its regular Earth observation series.

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:36.540
the observation support ongoing monitoring of coastal environments. NASA named

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:41.400
Kayla Barron as commander for the crew 14 mission. Chris Birch will serve as

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:47.620
pilot, Chaksa Astramat, Makoto Sua, and Ross Cosmos Cosmonaut Ivan Vagner

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:52.080
complete the four-person crew. The mission will travel to the International Space

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:58.980
Station. Launch is targeted for no earlier than spring 2027. Chinyare Ukehe of

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:02.220
Philadelphia received the top $300,000 prize.

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:04.780
The award recognized the adaptive

00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:06.800
nourishment infrastructure concept.

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:09.340
The challenge sought integrated systems

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:11.800
that grow, process, and prepare food

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:13.580
for long duration Mars missions.

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:16.740
Eight additional teams earned smaller awards

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:18.360
for complementary technologies.

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:20.860
The winning designs address nutrition

00:05:20.860 --> 00:05:23.200
and sustainability needs for future crews.

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:26.100
Carbon capture hardware originally developed

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:33.040
for Mars habitats is now purifying carbon dioxide in craft breweries. The technology removes unwanted

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:39.600
gases during fermentation. It improves consistency of the final product. Several breweries have adopted

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:45.120
the systems through NASA technology transfer agreements. The adaptation demonstrates practical

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:51.600
earth applications of space hardware. An Alta-X drone tested the safe and precise landing integrated

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:57.340
capability's evolution system. The test occurred near NASA Armstrong Flight Research Center.

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:02.200
The experiment simulated lunar descent profiles using sensors and algorithms.

00:06:02.980 --> 00:06:05.680
The algorithms were developed at Johnson Space Center.

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:10.700
Results will refine guidance software for future landers on the moon and other bodies.

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:16.720
NASA astronaut Victor Glover has signed with CAA after completing his NASA career

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:18.500
and joining the Emeritus program.

00:06:18.500 --> 00:06:25.860
The agency representation covers unscripted television, book deals, speaking engagements, and brand partnerships.

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:31.780
Glover's social media reach exceeds 780,000 followers across platforms.

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:36.820
His Artemis II flight made him the first black astronaut to travel around the moon.

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:42.500
The move opens new avenues for communicating spaceflight experiences to wider audiences,

00:06:42.980 --> 00:06:46.160
while he advises his alma mater on engineering programs.

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:46.320
times.

00:06:46.900 --> 00:06:50.240
Glover continues to support future missions through the Emeritus track.

00:06:51.160 --> 00:06:56.180
A poffis will pass Earth at a distance roughly one-tenth the average Earth moon separation.

00:06:56.820 --> 00:07:01.640
That closeness allows tidal forces to flex the asteroids crest by several millimeters.

00:07:02.420 --> 00:07:06.840
Picture a city block of solid rock bending like a steel beam under its own weight.

00:07:07.480 --> 00:07:11.720
That scale of deformation is expected during the April 2029 encounter.

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:16.960
encounter. Radio tracking and laser ranging from the Ramsey spacecraft will measure those

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:22.700
tiny shifts in real time. The measurements turn the flyby into a natural seismic experiment.

00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:28.340
The data should reveal whether a poffis is a single monolithic body or a loosely bound

00:07:28.340 --> 00:07:34.560
rubble pile. Current ground observations cannot resolve that question. Scientists still cannot

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:39.900
predict exactly how much energy will couple into surface motion. They also cannot say whether

00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:42.680
their dust will lift off and form a temporary coma.

00:07:43.300 --> 00:07:47.800
The unanswered question is whether the same tidal stresses that expose internal layers

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:52.820
also trigger unpredictable surface changes that could affect future deflection planning.

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:59.920
Before we go, keep an eye on the upcoming Starship Flight 14 test as preparations continue at Starbase.

